From db2962fd3ea8dc873101449514ed4f7ccd0d3464 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 20:19:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): bind the delegate-child env marker to its owner
 process; orphans heal [daveey]

Five whys (Desktop chats refused board writes 10-21 18:53-21:40 PT):
1. Hermes.app carried HERMES_DELEGATED_CHILD_CONTEXT.
2. A kanban worker's `hermes update` reopened it, and the env came with it.
3. Every worker subprocess carries the marker by design, and env vars are inherited by every
   descendant, including processes that leave the lineage (`open`, detached launches, ppid 1).
4. is_delegated_child_process_context() trusted the variable alone. Correctness depended on every
   launch site scrubbing it, and four scrub fixes landed within 80 minutes (4eff2691da, 9778500b6b,
   d5f8a69c87, f26e75a136). The next day a detached BabyBear (ppid 1) still carried the marker.
5. Root cause: the fence's authority was an ambient capability with no binding to the lineage it stood for.

Fix: scrub_kanban_env stamps HERMES_DELEGATED_CHILD_OWNER="<pid>:<start fingerprint>" of the agent
process that first fences a lineage (inherited owners are kept). The env marker counts only while
that exact process incarnation is alive AND an ancestor of the reader (cached 10 s). A process
outside the lineage drops the agent-scope family from its own env once (logged), so neither it nor
its children are fenced. An unbound legacy marker still fences (fail closed). The in-process
delegate_task contextvar is unchanged. Launch-site scrubbing stays as defence in depth.

Trade-off: a worker's background process orphaned after the worker exits is no longer fenced. It has
no live owner to protect.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
